  "enhanced_information": "Septic shock resulting from systemic infections due to Gram-negative bacteria is primarily caused by lipopolysaccharide (LPS), a major component of the outer membrane of these bacteria. LPS is recognized by immune cells, triggering a strong inflammatory response characterized by the release of pro-inflammatory cytokines. This cascade leads to widespread vasodilation, hypotension, and ultimately organ dysfunction, culminating in septic shock. The reasoning chains consistently highlight the role of LPS in this process, aligning with established medical consensus. Evidence paths confirming the presence of LPS in Gram-negative bacteria and its role in immune activation further support this understanding."
